CHECK_ID: W3B12:W3B12:CHK-0001
TITLE: Verify Trust Boundary Integrity: Can the slotted_freezable mechanism for Header objects be bypassed?

PHASE1_OUTPUT:
The go-ethereum Header struct has NO slotted_freezable mechanism - this is a Python-only concept from execution-specs. Go structs are inherently mutable. The codebase uses CopyHeader() for defensive copies. Two pointer leak anomalies found: Block.BeaconRoot() and Block.RequestsHash() return internal *common.Hash pointers without copying, inconsistent with other accessors (ExcessBlobGas, BlobGasUsed) which do copy. Current callers use read-only so not actively exploited. CopyHeader itself is complete for all 9 pointer/reference fields.

KEY_ANOMALIES:
- SA-001: Block.BeaconRoot() returns b.header.ParentBeaconRoot directly (pointer leak)
- SA-002: Block.RequestsHash() returns b.header.RequestsHash directly (pointer leak)
- SA-004: No freeze mechanism exists - structural mismatch with Python spec

CODE_EXCERPT:
```go
// core/types/block.go lines 410-412
func (b *Block) BeaconRoot() *common.Hash     { return b.header.ParentBeaconRoot }
func (b *Block) RequestsHash() *common.Hash   { return b.header.RequestsHash }

// Compare with defensive copy pattern used elsewhere:
func (b *Block) ExcessBlobGas() *uint64 {
	var excessBlobGas *uint64
	if b.header.ExcessBlobGas != nil {
		excessBlobGas = new(uint64)
		*excessBlobGas = *b.header.ExcessBlobGas
	}
	return excessBlobGas
}
